arXiv:2509.00691v1 [cs.CL] 31 Aug 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alex Gulko
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:gulko.5@osu.edu">gulko.5@osu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yusen Peng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:peng.1007@osu.edu">peng.1007@osu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sachin Kumar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Ohio State University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:kumar.1145@osu.edu">kumar.1145@osu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Probing with sparse autoencoders is a promising approach for uncovering interpretable features in large language models (LLMs). However, the lack of automated evaluation methods has hindered their broader adoption and development. In this work, we introduce CE-Bench, a novel and lightweight contrastive evaluation benchmark for sparse autoencoders, built on a curated dataset of contrastive story pairs. We conduct comprehensive ablation studies to validate the effectiveness of our approach. Our results show that CE-Bench reliably measures the interpretability of sparse autoencoders and aligns well with existing benchmarks—all without requiring an external LLM. The official implementation and evaluation dataset are open-sourced under the MIT License.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
      
      
    <sup id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Both authors contributed equally to this research.</span></span></span><span id="footnote1a" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
      
      
    Code: <a href="https://github.com/Yusen-Peng/CE-Bench" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Link</a>; Dataset: <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/GulkoA/contrastive-stories-v4" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Link</a></span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the core technical challenges in explaining large language models (LLMs) is the problem of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">polysemanticity</span>, or neuron superposition—the phenomenon where individual neurons are activated by semantically distinct inputs. This ambiguity undermines the ability to assign clear, interpretable meanings to individual neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023a)</a></cite>. Prior work has identified that this superposition arises when the number of latent features exceeds the number of neurons. A promising solution is to learn a sparse representation of the model’s internal activations using Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023a)</a></cite>. A sparse autoencoder is a variant of an autoencoder that enforces sparsity in its latent representations, making it well-suited for uncovering disentangled, human-interpretable features from various components of an LLM, such as attention heads, MLP layers, or residual streams. Typically, this is achieved by incorporating a sparsity-inducing regularization term into the reconstruction loss, encouraging the model to represent inputs as sparse linear combinations of learned features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. As a result, sparse autoencoders have gained popularity and been integrated into a variety of interpretability libraries and toolkits for LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pach et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For example, SAELens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joseph Bloom and Chanin (2024)</a></cite> supports four major SAE variants: the Standard SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, Gated SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajamanoharan et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, JumpReLU SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajamanoharan et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, and Top-K SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alongside their widespread adoption, sparse autoencoders have also been evaluated across a range of dimensions, including model steering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arad et al. (2025)</a></cite> and concept detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. SAEBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, for instance, offers a unified evaluation framework for reconstruction quality, interpretability, and feature disentanglement. For interpretability, it builds upon the idea of LLM-assisted simulation, using natural language explanations to probe neuron activations and derive evaluation metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bills et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Similarly, RouteSAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposes a simpler approach that feeds top neuron activations into an external LLM judge to produce interpretability scores. However, a major limitation shared by these approaches is their reliance on querying an external LLM during evaluation. This introduces non-determinism, potential biases, and a lack of reproducibility—issues that are only partially mitigated by repeated prompt trials. Critically, there is still no evaluation framework that can assess the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">interpretability</span> of sparse autoencoders in a completely <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-free</span> manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CE-Bench</span>, a novel, fully LLM-free contrastive evaluation benchmark. CE-Bench measures interpretability by analyzing neuron activation patterns across semantically contrastive contexts, thereby eliminating the noise and instability associated with LLM-based judgments. We construct a high-quality dataset comprising 5,000 contrastive story pairs across 1,000 distinct subjects, curated via structured WikiData queries and supplemented by human validation. Our evaluation demonstrates that CE-Bench reliably captures interpretability and closely aligns with results from LLM-assisted benchmarks—serving as a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">faithful, deterministic proxy</span> for interpretability evaluation. By avoiding the use of LLMs during evaluation, CE-Bench offers a more stable, reproducible, and transparent alternative for assessing sparse autoencoder performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/CE_Bench.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/151;" width="476" height="151" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of the CE-Bench pipeline.</span> Two contrastive stories about the same subject are passed through a frozen LLM and a pretrained sparse autoencoder (SAE) to extract neuron activations. A contrastive score is computed as the max absolute difference between the stories’ average activations (<math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), while an independence score measures deviation from the dataset-wide activation mean (<math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math>). These scores, along with SAE sparsity, are used to derive an interpretability score via either (1) supervised regression using SAE-Bench scores as ground truth, or (2) simple averaging for an LLM-free, deterministic evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Existing Interpretability Benchmarks</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Explanation-Based Simulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The explanation-based simulation framework—also known as <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Automated Interpretability</span>—was introduced by OpenAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bills et al. (2023)</a></cite> as a method for evaluating the interpretability of individual neurons in sparse autoencoders. In this approach, the input text and the activation values of a specific neuron are provided to an LLM, which is prompted to generate a short natural language explanation describing the neuron’s semantic behavior. To assess how well this explanation reflects the neuron’s behavior, a second LLM is used to simulate the original neuron activations based solely on the explanation. Both the original text and the generated explanation are fed into this second LLM, which is prompted to output simulated activation values on the same scale as the original neuron. Finally, the interpretability score is computed as the similarity (e.g., cosine similarity or R²) between the original and simulated activation vectors. A higher similarity suggests that the explanation accurately captures the neuron’s behavior, indicating stronger interpretability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Score-Based Hard Assignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RouteSAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposes a simpler alternative evaluation framework based on discrete score assignment using LLMs. For each neuron, a prompt is constructed that includes the top-activated tokens and their corresponding activation values. The LLM is instructed to categorize the neuron into one of three types: <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">low-level</span> (e.g., lexical or syntactic features), <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high-level</span> (e.g., semantic or long-range dependencies), or <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">indiscernible</span>. Additionally, the LLM assigns an integer interpretability score from 1 to 5, reflecting how coherent or meaningful the neuron’s behavior appears to be. During evaluation, interpretability scores are averaged over a set of top-activated neurons. This method provides a more direct but coarse-grained quantification of interpretability, with interpretability interpreted as a categorical judgment rather than a continuous similarity metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>CE-Bench</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce our contrastive evaluation framework, CE-Bench, illustrated in the pipeline and metric computation diagram in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Curated Dataset of Contrastive Stories</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support CE-Bench, we construct a high-quality, semi-automated dataset consisting of 5,000 pairs of contrastive stories across 1,000 distinct subjects. The dataset construction follows a two-stage filtering and synthesis process:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Subject Selection.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by scraping over 117 million entities from WikiData. A series of rule-based filters are applied to reduce the candidate set to approximately 16,000 entries. These filtering rules are designed to exclude overly obscure, abstract, or ambiguous entries, retaining only those that represent well-known concepts, ideas, or objects familiar to an average English speaker. From this reduced set, 1,000 subjects are randomly sampled and manually reviewed to ensure quality and conceptual clarity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contrastive Story Generation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each of the 1,000 curated subjects, we synthetically generate two semantically contrastive stories using GPT-4.1. These stories are created based on a carefully designed prompt (shown in Table <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Dataset Curation Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> in the Appendix). The prompt ensures that the two narratives about the same subject diverge significantly in perspective, context, or implication—while remaining grounded in the same core entity. For each subject, five story pairs are generated, yielding a total of 5,000 contrastive pairs. An illustrative example is provided in Table <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Contrastive Story Pair Example ‣ A.3 Dataset Curation Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Contrastive Score</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hypothesize that if a sparse autoencoder (SAE) has learned semantically meaningful features, then neurons associated with a particular subject should exhibit significantly different activation patterns when presented with two contrastive descriptions of that subject. In other words, greater divergence in neuron activations between semantically contrasting inputs implies higher interpretability of the latent space. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we formalize this intuition as follows. For each story pair, we compute the average neuron activations across all tokens in each story. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the resulting mean activation vectors for the two contrastive stories, respectively. To quantify the contrast, we compute the neuron-wise contrastive vector as the element-wise absolute difference between <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=|V_{1}-V_{2}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=|V_{1}-V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of the latent space. To summarize this vector into a single scalar contrastive score for the entire SAE, we apply a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">max pooling</span> operation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Contrastive Score}=\max(C)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Contrastive Score</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Contrastive Score}=\max(C)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This pooling strategy emphasizes the most responsive neuron—i.e., the one that exhibits the largest differential activation between the two stories. Our rationale is that this neuron is most likely to capture the semantic distinction introduced by the contrastive prompts. Hence, its behavior serves as a proxy for how well the autoencoder has disentangled interpretable features in its latent space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/sae_analysis.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of SAE Architecture on Interpretability.</span> CE-Bench interpretability scores show strong positive correlations with contrastive and independence scores, and a negative correlation with sparsity across SAE variants. Among all architectures, top-k and p-anneal consistently yield the highest interpretability, aligning closely with SAE-Bench ground truth.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Independence Score</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a complementary hypothesis: if the neuron activations corresponding to a specific semantic subject differ more significantly from the average behavior across all subjects, then the latent space of the sparse autoencoder (SAE) is likely to be more interpretable. Intuitively, interpretable neurons should respond uniquely to individual subjects rather than in a uniform or entangled manner. To evaluate this, we first compute the sum of the mean activation vectors for the two contrastive stories associated with a given subject:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1}=V_{1}+V_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1}=V_{1}+V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the average activation vectors of the two contrastive stories, as defined in the previous section. Next, we calculate the mean of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> across all <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5000</annotation></semantics></math> story pairs in our dataset:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{avg}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}I_{1}^{(i)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{avg}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}I_{1}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This global average vector <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a baseline representation of general neuron activity across the dataset. To assess the subject-specific deviation from this baseline, we compute the neuron-wise independence vector as the element-wise absolute difference between <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=|I_{1}-I_{\text{avg}}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=|I_{1}-I_{\text{avg}}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Finally, we derive a scalar independence score for the SAE by applying a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">max pooling</span> operation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Independence Score}=\max(D)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Independence Score</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Independence Score}=\max(D)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">This highlights the neuron that deviates most strongly from its dataset-wide average response—i.e., the neuron that is most sensitive or specialized with respect to the semantic subject under consideration. A higher independence score thus suggests that the SAE has learned more distinct, interpretable features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Proxy Learning or Simple Average?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compute the final interpretability score in CE-Bench, we explore two distinct aggregation strategies for combining the contrastive score and independence score, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Method 1: Proxy Learning (Supervised Regression).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our first approach, we position CE-Bench as a <span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">faithful proxy</span> for evaluating the interpretability of sparse autoencoders in alignment with the definitions established by SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Specifically, we train a linear regression model using three features: the contrastive score, the independence score, and the sparsity level of the sparse autoencoder. The sparsity values are drawn from the pretrained SAEs publicly released by SAELens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joseph Bloom and Chanin (2024)</a></cite>. The regression model is trained using the interpretability scores from SAE-Bench as ground truth supervision, thereby enabling CE-Bench to approximate those scores through deterministic computation. However, this approach comes at the cost of inheriting potential biases, prompt instability, and noise from the original LLM-based evaluations used by SAE-Bench. As such, although the method aligns with existing benchmarks, it is not fully independent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Method 2: Simple Average.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a cleaner alternative, we propose computing the final CE-Bench score as the simple arithmetic average of the contrastive and independence scores. This naive aggregation method avoids the complexities and potential drawbacks of proxy learning while preserving determinism and reproducibility. Although it lacks alignment with existing LLM-based benchmarks, it offers a more principled signal for interpretability evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative Comparison.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate both approaches—proxy learning and simple averaging—in the subsequent experimental section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>. Our results compare their consistency, alignment with SAE-Bench, and robustness across different SAE variants.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/width_analysis.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Latent Space Width on Interpretability.</span> CE-Bench interpretability scores increase consistently with latent space width, with the 65k-dimension models showing the highest contrastive and independence scores and the lowest sparsity. This suggests that wider latent spaces enable sparse autoencoders to better disentangle meaningful features and reduce polysemanticity.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Pretrained Sparse Autoencoders</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize a wide range of pretrained sparse autoencoders (SAEs) publicly released by SAE-Lens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joseph Bloom and Chanin (2024)</a></cite> and SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which cover multiple LLM backbones and SAE architectural variants. Rather than training SAEs from scratch, we rely on these pretrained models for two key reasons. First, it removes the substantial computational overhead associated with training, making it feasible to focus on benchmarking. Second, using standardized public models ensures a fair comparison and proxy learning setup between CE-Bench and existing benchmarks, particularly SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Train/Test Split for Proxy Learning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although CE-Bench offers two aggregation strategies for computing interpretability scores—proxy learning and simple averaging—we adopt the supervised <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proxy learning</span> approach as our primary method. The simple average is reserved for comparative analysis. To train the linear regression model described in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Proxy Learning or Simple Average? ‣ 3 CE-Bench ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we curate a training set of 48 pretrained SAEs for which SAE-Bench interpretability scores are available. This training set is used to fit the regression model and is analyzed in Sections <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Architecture of SAEs ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Width of Latent Space ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, focusing on variations across SAE architectures and latent space widths. For evaluation, we compile a disjoint test set of 45 pretrained SAEs whose SAE-Bench interpretability scores are not publicly available. These models are evaluated in Sections <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Type of LLM Layers ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Depth of LLM Layers ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, where we investigate interpretability across varying LLM layer depths and types. The exact train/test split is documented in our GitHub repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Correct Ranking Pair Ratio</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the reliability of CE-Bench after training with supervision from SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we introduce the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correct Ranking Pair Ratio (CRPR)</span>. This metric evaluates whether CE-Bench preserves the relative interpretability ranking of model pairs. For every pair of SAEs, we check whether the binary ranking between their predicted interpretability scores (from CE-Bench) matches the ranking given by SAE-Bench. A pair is marked as <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">concordant</span> if the rankings agree, and as <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discordant</span> otherwise. The CRPR is then computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CRPR}=\frac{\text{\# concordant pairs}}{\text{\# total pairs}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CRPR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext># concordant pairs</mtext><mtext># total pairs</mtext></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CRPR}=\frac{\text{\# concordant pairs}}{\text{\# total pairs}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A higher CRPR indicates better alignment with SAE-Bench rankings, demonstrating CE-Bench’s effectiveness as a proxy metric. Importantly, CRPR is not limited to the proxy learning approach—it can also be applied to the simple average strategy, allowing us to compare the relative consistency of both scoring methods. Additional experiments and results using CRPR are presented in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/layer_type_analysis.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/145;" width="381" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of LLM Layer Type on Interpretability.</span> CE-Bench predicted interpretability scores show consistent trends across attention, MLP, and residual stream layers with respect to contrastive score, independence score, and sparsity. The similarity in curves across layer types suggests that sparse autoencoder interpretability is not strongly influenced by the type of transformer sub-layer being probed.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our main empirical findings, evaluating the effectiveness of CE-Bench across a variety of experimental conditions. Specifically, we examine how CE-Bench responds to changes in the architecture of sparse autoencoders, the width of their latent space, the type of LLM layer being probed, and the depth of the layer within the LLM. Unless otherwise specified, all experiments use the proxy learning approach described in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Proxy Learning or Simple Average? ‣ 3 CE-Bench ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. A direct comparison between proxy learning and the simple average method is provided in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, using the Correct Ranking Pair Ratio (CRPR) defined in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Correct Ranking Pair Ratio ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. We also include visualizations of CE-Bench’s contrastive and independence scores to offer additional interpretability insights.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Architecture of SAEs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by evaluating CE-Bench on a set of 36 pretrained sparse autoencoders that probe the Gemma-2-2B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In this setting, all SAEs share a fixed latent dimensionality of 65,000 and target activations from the 12th residual stream layer. To ensure a fair comparison with SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we include sparse autoencoders drawn from six different architectural families: standard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, top-k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, p-anneal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2024)</a></cite>, batch-top-k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bussmann et al. (2024)</a></cite>, jumprelu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajamanoharan et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, gated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajamanoharan et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, and matryoshka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bussmann et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Contrastive Score ‣ 3 CE-Bench ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents our results. The y-axis reflects CE-Bench’s predicted interpretability scores. In the first three subplots, we examine the relationship between our predictions and the contrastive score, the independence score, and the sparsity of the SAE, all plotted on the x-axis. The results show that predicted interpretability scores are positively associated with the contrastive and independence scores, and negatively associated with the SAE’s sparsity level. In the fourth subplot, we compare our predicted scores with the automated interpretability scores from SAE-Bench, which serve as the ground truth for training. We observe strong alignment between CE-Bench predictions and SAE-Bench metrics, supporting the claim that CE-Bench can reliably approximate interpretability in a deterministic and efficient way. Among the SAE architectures considered, the top-k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024b)</a></cite> and p-anneal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2024)</a></cite> models consistently achieve the highest interpretability scores. This trend is consistent with findings from SAE-Bench and reinforces CE-Bench’s effectiveness in differentiating interpretability across architectural choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Width of Latent Space</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate CE-Bench on a set of 16 pretrained sparse autoencoders probing the Gemma-2-2B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Among these, four models overlap with the architecture-based experiment discussed in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Architecture of SAEs ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. For consistency, we fix the sparse autoencoder architecture to <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">jumprelu</span> and probe activations from the 12th residual stream layer. In this experiment, we vary the width of the latent space across three settings: 4k, 16k, and 65k. The first three subplots in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Quantitative Comparison. ‣ 3.4 Proxy Learning or Simple Average? ‣ 3 CE-Bench ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> present the corresponding contrastive scores, independence scores, and sparsity levels. Our results reveal a strong and consistent trend: wider latent spaces are associated with higher predicted interpretability scores from CE-Bench. This observation supports the hypothesis that sparse autoencoders require sufficiently large latent spaces to effectively resolve polysemanticity and capture distinct, interpretable features.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/depth_analysis.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/145;" width="381" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Layer Depth on Interpretability.</span> CE-Bench interpretability predictions across different LLM layer depths show that shallow layers (e.g., Layer 0 and Layer 5) consistently yield lower contrastive and independence scores, and thus lower interpretability. In contrast, deeper layers (Layers 10–25) achieve higher and more stable interpretability, suggesting that meaningful, disentangled features emerge later in the model.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Type of LLM Layers</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate how the type of LLM layer affects the interpretability of sparse autoencoders, we switch from the standard SAELens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joseph Bloom and Chanin (2024)</a></cite> and SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite> models—where such variation is limited—to a new suite of pretrained sparse autoencoders from the gemma-scope-2b collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In this setting, the latent space width is fixed at 16,000, and the SAE architecture is set to <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">jumprelu</span> for all models. We examine three types of transformer sub-layers within the 12th layer of the model: the attention layer, the MLP layer, and the residual stream layer. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Correct Ranking Pair Ratio ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the predicted interpretability scores from CE-Bench in relation to the contrastive score, independence score, and sparsity of each model. Since these models belong to the test set defined earlier in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Train/Test Split for Proxy Learning ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, SAE-Bench interpretability scores are not available, and thus no comparison to ground truth is shown. Our results suggest that the choice of layer type—attention, MLP, or residual—does not significantly affect the interpretability score as measured by CE-Bench. This indicates a level of robustness in sparse autoencoder performance across different types of internal LLM representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Depth of LLM Layers</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the limited availability of pretrained sparse autoencoders for the Gemma-2-2B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite> in SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we continue our experiments using the gemma-scope-2b suite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In this setting, we fix the SAE architecture to <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">jumprelu</span>, the latent space width to 16k, and the probed component to the residual stream. We vary the depth of the probed layer, evaluating the 0th, 5th, 10th, 15th, 20th, and 25th layers. Results are presented in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Width of Latent Space ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. As noted in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Train/Test Split for Proxy Learning ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, these models belong to the test split and thus do not have SAE-Bench interpretability scores, so no ground truth comparison is shown. Our results indicate that shallow layers—specifically the 0th and 5th—exhibit substantially lower predicted interpretability scores. In contrast, layers at greater depth (10th, 15th, 20th, and 25th) show consistently higher and comparable levels of interpretability. These findings suggest that meaningful, disentangled representations tend to emerge in later stages of LLM processing, whereas early layers may encode more general or less interpretable features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline</h3>

<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score Derivation method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CRPR<math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">proxy learning</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">75.98%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">simple average</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">70.12%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">average with sparsity penalty</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">74.82%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of Interpretability Score Derivation Methods via CRPR.</span> Proxy learning achieves the highest ranking agreement (75.98%) with SAE-Bench, but the unsupervised method using an average with a sparsity penalty (74.82%) also demonstrates CE-Bench’s robustness even without supervision.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/score_vis.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/119;" width="476" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Visualization of Neuron-wise Scores for the Subject “Computer.”</span> The left scatter plot shows each neuron’s contrastive and independent scores, with top-right points indicating neurons that are both highly contrastive and independent. The cyan and yellow highlighted neurons determine the sparse autoencoders final contrastive (109.27) and independent (195.90) scores via max pooling. The center and right histograms reveal that most neurons have low scores, suggesting that only a small subset of features are semantically relevant for the given subject.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As introduced in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Proxy Learning or Simple Average? ‣ 3 CE-Bench ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, CE-Bench supports two methods for aggregating contrastive and independence scores into a single interpretability score: (1) proxy learning, where a linear regression model is trained using SAE-Bench interpretability scores as ground truth, and (2) simple average, which computes the unweighted mean of the contrastive and independence scores without supervision. To evaluate the alignment between CE-Bench predictions and SAE-Bench ground truth, we use the Correct Ranking Pair Ratio (CRPR) metric described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Correct Ranking Pair Ratio ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, which quantifies how well the predicted ranking of sparse autoencoders matches the SAE-Bench reference ranking. As reported in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.5 Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proxy learning method achieves a CRPR of 75.98%, confirming its effectiveness as a supervised proxy. Surprisingly, the simple average baseline also performs competitively, achieving a CRPR of 70.12%, despite the absence of any learned parameters or labeled supervision. Building on this observation, we hypothesize that incorporating the sparsity of the sparse autoencoder as a regularizing signal may further improve unsupervised performance. Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cunningham et al. (2023a)</a></cite> has documented the tradeoff between sparsity and reconstruction quality, and our own results (Sections <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Architecture of SAEs ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>–<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Depth of LLM Layers ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>) consistently show a negative correlation between sparsity and interpretability. Motivated by this, we construct a third unsupervised variant that computes the average of the contrastive and independence scores, then subtracts sparsity as a penalty term. This adjusted unsupervised method achieves a CRPR of 74.82%, nearly matching the supervised proxy learning approach. These findings demonstrate that <span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">even without supervised training</span>, CE-Bench can reliably approximate interpretability rankings, making it a robust and reproducible proxy for SAE-Bench.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Sample Score Visualization: Neuron-wise Contrastive Score and Independent Score</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide deeper insight into how CE-Bench computes interpretability scores, we visualize the distributions of neuron-wise contrastive and independent scores, as well as their joint relationship. These visualizations help clarify the role of the max pooling operation used to summarize neuron-wise metrics into a single scalar score per sparse autoencoder. For each contrastive story pair in our dataset, we generate three diagnostic plots: the distribution of neuron-wise contrastive scores, the distribution of neuron-wise independent scores, and a scatter plot that places each neuron in a 2D space defined by its contrastive and independent scores. In the scatter plot, neurons in the upper-right quadrant are both highly contrastive and highly independent, indicating a strong subject-specific activation pattern.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As an example, Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.5 Comparative Study: Proxy Learning vs. Simple Average Baseline ‣ 5 Results ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents these plots for the first contrastive story pair in our curated dataset, where the semantic subject is <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">computer</span>. The leftmost scatter plot shows that only a small subset of neurons have high contrastive or independent scores. The majority of neurons lie near the origin, suggesting weak or non-specific activation for this subject. CE-Bench performs max pooling to extract the most semantically responsive neuron for each metric. Specifically, the <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2C7C92;">rightmost cyan neuron</span> in the scatter plot, which exhibits the highest neuron-wise contrastive score, determines the final contrastive score for the sparse autoencoder: 109.2734. Similarly, the <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B3B303;">topmost yellow neuron</span> defines the independent score: 195.9004. The accompanying histograms confirm that most neurons contribute minimally, reinforcing CE-Bench’s ability to isolate interpretable, high-signal dimensions in the sparse latent space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced CE-Bench, a fully <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-free</span>, contrastive evaluation framework for measuring the <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">interpretability</span> of sparse autoencoders. By leveraging contrastive and independent neuron activation scores, CE-Bench offers a stable, deterministic, and reproducible alternative to LLM-based interpretability methods such as Automated Interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bills et al. (2023)</a></cite>. To support this benchmark, we curated a dataset of 5,000 contrastive story pairs across 1,000 semantic subjects. Through extensive ablation studies, we demonstrated CE-Bench’s robustness across different SAE architectures, latent widths, LLM layer types, and depths. Our results show that CE-Bench closely aligns with SAE-Bench rankings—even in fully unsupervised settings—establishing it as a reliable <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">proxy metric</span> for interpretability evaluation. We hope CE-Bench will serve as a useful tool for future research in probing, interpreting, and improving the internal representations of large language models.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
