<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Broader Impact</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CE-Bench offers a compelling alternative to existing interpretability evaluation methods for sparse autoencoders, particularly by eliminating reliance on external LLM judges. Its design emphasizes <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">determinism</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scalability</span>, and <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reproducibility</span>, addressing core limitations in LLM-based methods such as prompt sensitivity, generation noise, and resource overhead. Our experiments demonstrate that CE-Bench captures key properties of interpretable neurons: responsiveness to semantic contrast, deviation from dataset-wide averages, and low redundancy. These patterns hold consistently across diverse sparse autoencoder designs and probing conditions, reinforcing the generality of our evaluation framework. A particularly encouraging result is CE-Bench’s ability to approximate SAE-Bench interpretability rankings even without supervision. The success of the sparsity-penalized averaging method suggests that meaningful interpretability signals can be recovered from model-internal statistics alone, opening the door to broader use in low-resource or experimental settings where no ground truth is available.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct the following two ablation studies to guide us in making optimal decisions for the CE-Bench pipeline. We experiment with two alternative pooling strategies to derive SAE metrics from neuron-wise scores; we also experiment with different sets of feature selection for linear regression training.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.1 </span>Pooling Strategy</h4>

<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pooling strategy</span></th>
<th id="A1.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CRPR<math id="A1.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">max pooling</th>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A1.T3.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">75.98%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">mean pooling</th>
<td id="A1.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">71.01%</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">outlier count outside of <math id="A1.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A1.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">72.34%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="A1.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of Pooling Strategies via CRPR.</span> Max pooling achieves the highest Correct Ranking Pair Ratio (CRPR) at 75.98%, outperforming mean pooling and the outlier count method. This supports max pooling as the most effective strategy for aggregating neuron-wise scores.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study to evaluate the effect of different pooling strategies in CE-Bench’s final step, which aggregates neuron-wise scores into a single interpretability score for each sparse autoencoder (SAE). This aggregation is critical for ensuring that CE-Bench reliably reflects interpretability. In addition to the default <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">max pooling</span> strategy, we explore two alternatives: 1. Mean pooling, where the average of all neuron-wise scores is used as the SAE-level score. 2. Outlier count beyond one standard deviation (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>), where we count the number of neurons whose scores lie outside one standard deviation from the mean, using this count as a proxy for interpretability.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">qualitative analysis</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ A.2.2 Supervised Training Features ‣ A.2 Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, mean pooling performs poorly, exhibiting no meaningful correlation between CE-Bench predictions and the contrastive score. This suggests that averaging dilutes the influence of highly informative neurons. Similarly, Figure <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.2.2 Supervised Training Features ‣ A.2 Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows that the outlier-count method results in a strong negative correlation between CE-Bench predictions and the independent score, contradicting our core assumption: neurons that show larger activation differences from the dataset-wide average should be more interpretable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">quantitative comparison</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To complement this qualitative analysis, we also conduct a <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quantitative comparison</span> using the Correct Ranking Pair Ratio (CRPR) defined in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Correct Ranking Pair Ratio ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. As summarized in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2.1 Pooling Strategy ‣ A.2 Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CE-Bench: Towards a Reliable Contrastive Evaluation Benchmark of Interpretability of Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, max pooling achieves the highest CRPR, outperforming both mean pooling and the outlier-count method. Based on both empirical trends and theoretical consistency with our interpretability hypothesis, we conclude that <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">max pooling</span> is the most appropriate aggregation strategy for CE-Bench.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.2 </span>Supervised Training Features</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a systematic ablation study to evaluate the impact of different feature combinations used during supervised training for proxy learning. As a baseline, we begin with a model that uses only the <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sparsity</span> of the sparse autoencoder as its sole input feature, excluding all CE-Bench-specific components.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then incrementally incorporate the <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contrastive score</span> and <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">independent score</span>. When both scores are included alongside sparsity, we achieve the highest alignment with SAE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karvonen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, correctly ranking 75.98% of model pairs according to the CRPR metric. Removing either the contrastive score or the independent score leads to a slight decline in ranking performance, though both remain above 75%, indicating that each metric contributes complementary interpretability information.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To further explore the interaction between contrastive and independent signals, we define a new <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">joint score</span> by summing the neuron-wise contrastive and independent scores and applying max pooling. However, incorporating this joint score into the feature set results in a reduced CRPR of 74.29%, suggesting that this combined signal may introduce redundancy or noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we assess the contribution of sparsity by removing it from the feature set entirely. This leads to a substantial performance drop, with CRPR falling to 68.17%, which is even lower than the sparsity-only baseline. This result confirms that sparsity is a critical feature for accurately estimating the interpretability of sparse autoencoders and should not be excluded from the supervised training feature space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/ablation_mean.png" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A1.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation — Mean Pooling Strategy.</span> Using mean pooling results in highly inconsistent and noisy predictions, with no clear correlation between CE-Bench scores and the contrastive or independent metrics. This indicates that averaging across all neurons fails to highlight the most semantically informative features.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00691v1/ablation_outlier_count.png" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="A1.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation — Outlier Count Pooling Strategy.</span> This strategy yields a misleading negative correlation between predicted interpretability and independent scores, contradicting CE-Bench’s core assumption that high subject-specific activation deviation implies stronger interpretability. Thus, outlier count proves suboptimal.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
